                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the threat: two faces, one crisis`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `At 16:40 the journalist's email lands: 48 hours to a story alleging your £40M flagship overstated carbon-savings and that a junior analyst was pressured to “round up”. The leak is internal. Within three hours the anonymous channel says <em>“leadership knew”</em> and <em>“heads will roll”</em>. Two threats are forming at once — an <strong>external reputational</strong> one and an <strong>internal trust-and-wellbeing</strong> one — and the leak <strong>couples them</strong>. The trap is to treat them as separate, sequential problems, or to assume you already know the facts. Before any public word: separate what is <strong>true, contested and unknown</strong>, and quietly check whether the analyst really was pressured.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Choose the public posture, with the clock running`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `The story runs in hours. Choose the firm's public posture and match it to your <em>actual</em> responsibility (SCCT). A flat denial before you have verified the facts is the classic trap: if your own review later finds the analyst really was pressured, your denial — not the original error — becomes the story. Roulet is blunt: it's rarely the misconduct that stigmatises you, it's the <strong>cover-up</strong>. Draft a line your internal investigation can stand behind, decide what to tell the £26M public-sector clients before they read it in the paper, and enforce <strong>internal-external consistency</strong> — the discipline that keeps reputation and trust from collapsing together.`,
