                    warn_recovery_range:    `Wellbeing investment must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_coupled:       `<strong>That's the right frame.</strong> The leak came from inside — the rumour mill is shaping what your people tell their friends and the journalist's next source. The external and internal crises are one crisis with two faces; fight them together or each will feed the other.`,
                    coach_r1_external:      `<strong>You're fighting one face of a two-faced crisis.</strong> Fix the press today, deal with the team next week — but the engagement score is already sliding and the channel is authoring your story right now. Why are you fighting them one at a time?`,
                    coach_r1_internal:      `<strong>Hunting the leaker won't make the story go away.</strong> Treating this as purely an HR matter ignores the £40M reputational exposure — and threatening leakers will only confirm the “heads will roll” rumour and accelerate the spread.`,
                    coach_r1_overblown:     `<strong>Calling it overblown is how 28% becomes the headline.</strong> One journalist with internal emails and an anonymous source is a live negative evaluation. Ride it out and the silence becomes the story — both externally and internally.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named it as one coupled crisis AND committed to verify before speaking. Now you can choose a public posture your own review can stand behind — and protect the people the leak put in the firing line.`,
                    coach_r1_assumefalse:   `<strong>Careful — you're building a rebuttal before you know the facts.</strong> If you assume the methodology is sound and your own review later finds the analyst was pressured, you've set up the cover-up. It's rarely the misconduct that stigmatises you — it's the denial.`,
                    coach_r1_assumetrue:    `<strong>Conceding everything pre-emptively isn't transparency, it's panic.</strong> You don't yet know what is true, contested or unknown. Over-confessing unverified claims damages the named analyst and the client before any facts are in.`,
                    coach_r1_ignore:        `<strong>“Knowing less protects deniability” is exactly the cover-up instinct.</strong> Wilful blindness doesn't survive a journalist holding internal emails. You can't choose a defensible public line if you've refused to learn the facts.`,
                    coach_r1_noattention:   `<strong>You've put all your attention on the press.</strong> Meanwhile the anonymous channel is filling the silence — by lunchtime it has spread to most of the team. Gossip doesn't wait for your statement; from hour one it needs managing too.`,
                    coach_r2_acknowledge:   `<strong>Acknowledge-and-investigate matches your actual responsibility.</strong> Confirming an independent review and committing to publish findings is a posture your own investigation can stand behind — and it signals integrity to clients and staff alike.`,
                    coach_r2_limited:       `<strong>A limited statement is defensible but cautious.</strong> Taking it seriously while saying little buys time, but if you never commit to a visible, independent review the silence reads as evasion and the rumour mill fills the gap.`,
                    coach_r2_deny:          `<strong>You've denied everything publicly.</strong> Then your own review finds the analyst really was pressured — and the journalist has the internal emails. Your denial is now the story, not the original error. Can your public line survive your own investigation?`,
                    coach_r2_nocomment:     `<strong>“No comment” cedes the narrative entirely.</strong> The story runs unanswered, the clients hear only the accusation, and internally it confirms that leadership is hiding something. Silence externally is as costly as silence internally.`,
                    coach_r2_clientsilence: `<strong>Letting the clients read it in the paper is a needless wound.</strong> The £26M public-sector relationships run on trust and references; learning of a scandal cold makes a procurement freeze far likelier. Pre-brief them honestly.`,
                    coach_r2_inconsistent:  `<strong>Your public line and your internal reality are drifting apart.</strong> The moment the external message contradicts what the review finds, the leak widens, the story names the firm, and reputation and trust collapse together. Enforce consistency.`,
                    coach_r2_coverup:       `<strong>A flat denial you can't substantiate is the worst of both worlds.</strong> You've staked your credibility on a line your own investigation may demolish. This is precisely how an error becomes a scandal — the cover-up, not the conduct.`,
                    coach_r3_early:         `<strong>Briefing the 48 early and honestly is how you take back the narrative.</strong> Name the allegation, the review, and what you can't yet say. You don't have to know everything to be honest about what you do and don't know — and that honesty starves the rumour mill.`,
                    coach_r3_late:          `<strong>Waiting until the story runs lets the channel author it first.</strong> By the time you explain, the team has already absorbed “leadership knew” and “the analyst is being sacrificed”. You're now correcting a narrative instead of setting one.`,
                    coach_r3_silent:        `<strong>You told the team nothing to “keep it tight”.</strong> By lunchtime the anonymous channel filled the silence for you. You didn't control the narrative — you abandoned it. Who's writing your story now? Silence doesn't stop gossip; it feeds it.`,
                    coach_r3_over:          `<strong>Transparency isn't the same as broadcasting.</strong> Telling the whole team every suspicion and unproven claim during a live review creates a defamation risk, contaminates the investigation, and strips the analyst's privacy. What did the team actually need to know?`,
                    coach_r3_shutdown:      `<strong>Hunting the leaker confirms every fear.</strong> Threatening consequences for posting tells a speak-up culture that speaking up is dangerous — it accelerates the spread and torches the psychological safety that is your real asset.`,
                    coach_r3_ignore:        `<strong>It won't burn itself out.</strong> An unanswered channel doesn't cool — it compounds, and it feeds the journalist's next source. Ignoring the rumour mill is just a slower form of silence.`,
                    coach_r3_scapegoat:     `<strong>You framed it as one analyst's error and distanced leadership.</strong> Your two seniors — £1.4M of billing — built their loyalty on the belief that this place protects its people. They've just watched you not. You saved the practice's name and lost the team that makes the name worth anything.`,
                    coach_r3_prejudge:      `<strong>Pre-judging to settle the rumour breaks due process.</strong> Publicly clearing or condemning the analyst before the review concludes is unfair to them and undermines the legitimacy of whatever you decide later. Protect the person and the process.`,
                    coach_r3_broadcast:     `<strong>Maxing out disclosure broadcasts unproven claims.</strong> During a live investigation, “tell everyone everything” isn't courage — it's a defamation and privacy risk that the team didn't need and the analyst can't recover from.`,
                    coach_r3_silentpace:    `<strong>Near-total silence leaves the channel in charge.</strong> Slowing rumour means saying enough, honestly and early. The lowest-disclosure setting is exactly what hands the narrative to the anonymous channel.`,
                    coach_r4_honest:        `<strong>Honest commitments are how you actually keep people.</strong> Fair process and a real say in the fix — and no promises you can't keep — give the two seniors a reason to stay that survives contact with the review's findings.`,
                    coach_r4_overpromise:   `<strong>You promised no consequences and a guaranteed outcome.</strong> You can't honour either. When the review lands somewhere else, you've lied to the very people you were trying to keep — twice the betrayal. What could you have committed to that was actually true?`,
                    coach_r4_nothing:       `<strong>Assuming loyalty will hold is how you lose £1.4M of billing.</strong> The seniors are wavering precisely because they're watching what you do under pressure. Offer them something real — fair process and a voice — or they update their CVs tonight.`,
                    coach_r4_fair:          `<strong>A fair, transparent decision signals to the other 47.</strong> Acting on the evidence, protecting the person, and naming the systemic lesson is what tells the team the speak-up culture is real — that the leak was met with fairness, not retaliation.`,
                    coach_r4_quiet:         `<strong>Handling it quietly leaves the rumour unanswered.</strong> Resolving the analyst's situation in private, with the team told nothing, lets the channel keep guessing. Fairness that no one can see doesn't rebuild trust.`,
                    coach_r4_blame:         `<strong>Making a visible example is scapegoating in a suit.</strong> Disciplining to “show the firm acted” punishes the speak-up culture and tells the other 47 that exposure means sacrifice. You'll buy a headline and lose the team.`,
                    coach_r4_cosmetic:      `<strong>A policy refresh and a town-hall change little.</strong> Cosmetic governance signals motion without integrity; clients and staff can tell theatre from a real fix. Prove the methodology can't be quietly “rounded up” again.`,
                    coach_r4_nogov:         `<strong>“It was a one-off” is how it recurs.</strong> Without an independent sign-off, an audit trail and a protected challenge route, you've fixed nothing — and the next dispute lands on the same fault line.`,
                    coach_r4_norecovery:    `<strong>You can't rebuild trust on a memo.</strong> Without genuine listening, workload relief and restored psychological safety, the engagement score keeps sliding — the recovery is the work, not an afterthought.`,

