                    insight_clarity:        `Crisis turned to clarity: you held reputation, kept the team's trust and contained the rumour at once. Roulet's point made real — a negative evaluation, handled with consistency and care, clarified and even strengthened the practice rather than corroding it.`,
                    insight_coverup:        `Cover-up trap: at some point your public line ran ahead of what your own facts could support. It's rarely the original error that stigmatises an organisation — it's the denial. The reputational damage you took was self-inflicted by the gap between message and reality.`,
                    insight_scapegoat:      `You protected the name and lost the people who made it worth anything. Distancing leadership from the analyst broke the speak-up culture; the two seniors who built their loyalty on “this place protects its people” have gone, and £1.4M of billing with them.`,
                    insight_gossip:         `The rumour mill won. Silence and shutdown attempts let the anonymous channel author your story — to the team, and to the journalist's next source. Gossip moves faster than any press release; you ceded the narrative instead of shaping it.`,
                    insight_wellbeing:      `Wellbeing and trust ended fragile. In a scandal, protecting the exposed analyst and team isn't soft — it's the determinant of whether the team holds together. The psychological-safety asset you started with has been spent down.`,
                    insight_retained:       `You kept the two senior consultants and their £1.4M of billing — because you offered commitments you could actually honour, not promises that would betray them twice. Credible, honest retention beats reassurance you can't deliver.`,
                    insight_mixed:          `A mixed outcome: you survived the news cycle but left value on the table. Re-examine where your public line, your internal candour, and your care for the analyst could have been more tightly aligned.`,

                    hint_coverup:           `Your public line is running ahead of your verified facts — the cover-up trap. Realign the external message with what your review can actually stand behind before the gap becomes the story.`,
                    hint_scapegoat:         `You've distanced leadership from the analyst. The speak-up culture and your two seniors are watching — protect the person and the process, or trust collapses with the brand.`,
                    hint_gossip:            `The rumour mill is winning. Silence and shutdown only accelerate it — brief the team early and honestly, and answer the “leadership knew” rumour directly.`,
                    hint_wellbeing:         `Wellbeing and trust are dangerously low. Protect the named analyst with due process and invest in genuine re-engagement, or the team won't hold together.`,
                    hint_reputation:        `Reputation is sliding. A posture matched to your real responsibility — acknowledge and investigate, consistently — recovers standing faster than denial or silence.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — reputation held, trust preserved, rumour contained. Now lock it in with a real governance fix and honest retention so the recovery lasts.`,

